[1/6] Executive summary
  Synthesized two-stream model: activity_actions for attempted operations,
  activity_state_changes for accepted business facts. Business tables become
  projections. Optimistic locking moves to ETag/If-Match.

[2/6] Scope definition
  In scope: canonical split, append-only authority, ETag locking, clean
  business tables, actor attribution, indexing model, migration impact.
  Out of scope: implementation, migrations, UI surfaces, hybrid versioning.

[3/6] Canonical model and authority boundaries
  Defined action vs state change semantic split.
  Source of truth: state-change stream for history, action stream for attempts.
  Three storage layers: activity_actions, activity_state_changes, aggregate_heads.

[4/6] Write model and optimistic locking
  Concurrency contract via ETag/If-Match. Head-hash model with hash-chain.
  Canonical write path: validate → read head → compare If-Match → append action
  → append state changes → update heads → update projection → return ETag.
  Multi-aggregate actions supported, concurrency remains aggregate-scoped.

[5/6] Query model and indexing
  Three query classes: current business reads, concurrency checks, audit/history.
  Specified indexes on activity_actions, activity_state_changes, aggregate_heads.
  Projection tables keep business indexes only.

[6/6] Migration impact and downstream implications
  Chapters API: version removed, ETag added, 409→412, created_at becomes
  history metadata. Single cutover, no backwards-compatibility shim.
  Downstream: every mutable aggregate needs projection + head strategy.

